From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Fri, 28 Aug 2026 22:33:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 10202/11362] Record the 143 ERROR node ids the baseline was
 missing

An independent review closed two fail-open holes in the node-id delta today and
could only half-close the second. `failure_node_ids_from_pytest_output` read
only `FAILED ` lines, so a change that turns a PASS into a setup ERROR
introduces no failing node id and passes silently -- the same suppression class
the ledger exists to catch, re-shipped inside the fix for it.

It could not enforce that, because the baseline recorded the run's 1,726 FAILED
nodes and not the 143 ERROR nodes the same run saw. Enforcing against an empty
set would have refused every honest run, so delta() reported
error_nodes_assessed=false out loud and waited for a re-baseline.

No re-baseline was needed. Run 3's log still had them, so both ledgers now come
from the SAME measurement rather than two runs of a suite that takes four
hours. 143 recorded, matching the run's own reported count exactly, and
verified disjoint from the 1,726 FAILED nodes -- zero overlap.

Verified live: clean replay -> ready=True with error_nodes_assessed=True; one
new error node -> ready=False. 24 focused tests pass, including the debt
ratchet, which the added keys did not disturb.

Extraction note for whoever re-baselines next: `grep '^ERROR '` alone is wrong,
because pytest log output also begins with that token. The node ids must be
filtered to `^tests/python/.*\.py::` shape -- my first pass captured watchdog
log lines instead and would have poisoned the ledger with 17 non-nodes.

